{
  "title": "Boiling water cooks slowly up a mountain. So why does corn pop? | So That's Why",
  "description": "Little Q goes camping in the mountains: the water is clearly boiling, yet the noodles stay hard for ages. Back home, microwave popcorn pops all at once. Both just heat water, so why are the results so different?\n\nIn this episode, the Chief and Little Q follow the same drop of water: first, why boiling water has a ceiling it can't heat past, and why mountain air lowers that ceiling; then they shut the water inside a corn kernel's hard hull, to see how it heats past 100 degrees and holds on until the hull bursts; and finally, with a 2015 high-speed camera study, they pull apart the hull cracking, the jump and the \"pop\" as three separate things.\n\nThe illustrations in this episode were made with AI; they are diagrams, not measurements. The boiling points, popping temperature and pressure in the video are approximate figures from teaching materials and research. The pressure cooker is only mentioned in the video; please don't try pressure experiments at home. The source of every claim is listed in the references below.\n\nInformation as of 2026-11-07",
  "tags": [
    "popcorn",
    "boiling point",
    "high-altitude cooking",
    "air pressure",
    "Yushan",
    "science for kids",
    "So That's Why"
  ],
